  "question": {
    "text": "<p>A solid sphere of radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi></math> gravitationally attracts a particle placed at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mi>R</mi></math> from its centre with a force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>.</mo></math> Now a spherical cavity of radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mfrac><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mfenced></math> is made in the sphere (as shown in figure) and the force becomes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>. The value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>:</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> is:</p><p><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_main/752e8432-e36d-4c02-b770-3703f2eb95d5-image.png\" style=\"width: 278px; height: 163px;\" /></p>",
